{
  "dom_id": "step-q94061",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,G=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,H=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $F$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $F$ is not symmetric since ${f}_{12}\\ne {f}_{21}.$\n\n- $G$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $H$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix (not $2\\times 2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$G$ only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
